"Tapin" <navratil@gmail.com> writes:
> How does a first-timer know...
> ...that touching a 'trice is bad?

Read the Guidebook, revealing the availability of the '/' (whatis)
command. Use said command on a 'trice, by either name or location.
Observe the sentence which says:

   But even a dead basilisk is dangerous, for it is said that merely
   touching its lifeless body can cause a person to sicken and die.

> ...that wielding a 'trice is good, unless you fall into a pit or down
> stairs?

Posit the existence of some object such that the merest touch of said
object is potentially lethal. Might not such an object be a potent
weapon? Might it also pose significant risks to the wielder?

> ...that eating a green slime is bad?  (I still don't understand this
> one)

In general, the set things you can gainfully eat in nethack in
considerably larger than the set of things real-life experience
suggests it might be safe to eat. IOW, rather than asking "How would a
n00b know to avoid eating slime?" it would be better to ask "How would
a n00b know it's okay to eat fresh jackal corpses?" After all, would
*you* eat a "decaying green substance" you just found in an unlabelled
can of uncertain provenance?

A careful unspoiled player might consider watching what his dog or cat
eats as a conservative guide...

> ...that standing next to a drawbridge while doing anything to it is
> likely going to result in death?

A huge, heavy piece of dark-ages machinery under the control of
intelligent adversaries? Is it really that much of a stretch to
imagine that's a good thing to not stand directly in front of?

> ...that standing *on* a drawbridge can lead to death?

Okay, that one _is_ a little gratuitous and mean.

> ...that your Quest Nemesis wielding the Tsurugi of Muramasa can kill
> you even if you've got 350hps left?

/, n, tsurugi

   The tsurugi is rumored to be so sharp that it can occasionally cut
   opponents in half!

> ...that fighting a mind-flayer while burdened can kill you even if
> you've got 350hps left?

/, n, mind flayer

   If they hit their victim with a tentacle, the mind flayer will
   slowly drain it of all intelligence, eventually killing its victim.

Aside from which, if you manage to accrue 350HP without knowing how
a mind flayer works, you're clearly a very special newbie indeed.

> ...that falling into a pit or getting hit by a dart can kill you even
> if you've got 350hps left if you don't have poison resistance?

Appeal to genre convention? Traps and potentially lethal poison present
as a feature of ditto should not come as a shock to anyone with passing
familiarity with tabletop RPGs or pulp fantasy lit.

> etc, etc, etc.

Y'know, on this very same 'froup we right now have people complaining
that nethack is too easy?


